LONG.update({
'album-design-mistakes':('Album Design Mistakes to Avoid (and How to Fix Them)',[
"Most album complaints are not about the photos. They are about a cut-off face, a misspelt name, a page that looks darker than the rest or a design that feels crowded. These mistakes are common and almost all of them are easy to prevent. Here are the ones album designers see most often, with fixes.",
"## 1. Faces on the centre fold",
"On a 12x36 spread the fold sits at 18 inches. In thicker books the binding hides a strip around it, and even in lay-flat albums a face across the fold looks distorted. Drag a guide to the centre on every sheet and keep faces and names at least half an inch away from it.",
"## 2. Ignoring bleed and trim",
"Labs trim a small amount from each edge. Text or faces placed right at the edge can be cut. Keep at least 0.25 inch of bleed where only background continues, and a further safe margin for important content. Our [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/) covers this step by step.",
"## 3. Filling every frame",
"Templates often have many frames, but you do not have to use them all. Six mediocre photos on a spread look worse than one strong hero image with two supporting shots. Delete or hide frames you cannot fill with good pictures.",
"## 4. Too many fonts and styles",
"Different fonts, ornaments and colour schemes on every page make an album feel like unrelated templates. Pick one font pair and one template family per event. See [choosing fonts for wedding albums](/blog/fonts-for-wedding-albums/).",
"## 5. Enlarging small photos",
"Placing a cropped phone photo or a small JPG into a large frame gives soft, blocky prints. Check the Smart Object's scale in the Properties panel; above 100 percent means you are enlarging. Use the original high-resolution file, or put that photo in a smaller frame.",
"## 6. Inconsistent colour",
"Skin tones that change from page to page are very noticeable. Correct photos in batches per lighting situation before design, then fine-tune inside spreads with clipped adjustment layers. Our [colour correction tips](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/) explain the process.",
"## 7. Editing on a bright, uncalibrated screen",
"Prints that come back too dark are usually the result of editing on a laptop at full brightness. Lower brightness, calibrate if you can, and order a test print before printing a whole album at a new lab.",
"## 8. Leftover template elements",
"Sample text such as 'Your Name Here', hidden sample photos, watermark layers and guide layers sometimes survive into print. Before export, expand every group, delete what you do not need and check the flattened copy at 100 percent zoom.",
"## 9. Wrong colour mode",
"Converting photo album sheets to CMYK usually makes reds, maroons and golds look flat. Most photo-printed albums are produced from sRGB files. Read [RGB vs CMYK for photo albums](/blog/rgb-vs-cmyk-for-albums/) before you change modes.",
"## 10. No client approval in writing",
"Changing the order of events or replacing photos after printing is expensive. Share a low-resolution preview of every spread, ask the client to check names, dates and family members, and get approval in writing before sending files to the lab.",
"## 11. Poor file naming",
"Files named 'final', 'final2' and 'final new' lead to the wrong version being printed. Number sheets in order (01, 02, 03) and keep one Print folder that contains only the approved files.",
"## A habit that prevents most of these",
"Before export, hide panels with Tab, zoom to fit, and look at each spread for ten seconds as the client would. Then zoom to 100 percent and scan from left to right. Combined with a written checklist, this catches nearly every mistake on this list. When you are ready to design, start with a balanced layout from the [wedding album design PSD collection](/wedding-album-design-psd/)."]),
'12x30-vs-12x36-album':('12x30 vs 12x36 Album: Which Size Should You Choose?',[
"12x36 and 12x30 are the two most common panoramic album sizes offered by Indian studios and labs. Both are 12 inches tall; the difference is the width of the open spread. Choosing between them affects the look of the album, the price and how you design each page.",
"## The basic numbers",
"- **12x36:** each closed page is 12x18 inches, the open spread is 36 inches wide. At 300 DPI a sheet is 10800 x 3600 pixels.",
"- **12x30:** each closed page is 12x15 inches, the open spread is 30 inches wide. At 300 DPI a sheet is 9000 x 3600 pixels.",
"The 12x36 spread has a 3:1 aspect ratio; 12x30 is 2.5:1. That difference changes how layouts fit.",
"## How the album looks and feels",
"A 12x36 album feels grand and cinematic when opened, and it gives more room for a wide hero photo plus several supporting images. It is also larger and heavier to hold. A 12x30 album is a little more compact and easier to handle, and the slightly taller proportion suits portrait-oriented photos better.",
"## Cost",
"Pricing is set by each lab, but because a 12x30 sheet uses less paper than a 12x36 sheet, many labs price 12x30 albums lower for the same number of sheets. If a client's budget is tight, offering 12x30 with the same number of sheets is often better than cutting sheets from a 12x36 album. Ask your lab for its current rate card for both sizes; we do not publish prices because they vary widely by lab, paper and binding.",
"## Designing for each size",
"Do not stretch or squash a 12x36 layout to make it fit 12x30. Circles become ovals and photos look distorted. Either use templates built at the right proportions or resize the canvas properly. Our [12x30 album PSD collection](/12x30-album-psd/) contains sheets built at true 12x30 proportions, and the [12x36 collection](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) covers the larger format.",
"If you must convert a design, change the canvas size (Image > Canvas Size) rather than the image size, then rearrange frames and backgrounds to fill the new width. Our guide on [resizing album PSDs to another size](/blog/resize-album-psd-to-another-size/) explains the method.",
"## Which photos suit which size?",
"- Lots of wide group photos and landscapes: 12x36 gives them room.",
"- Mostly vertical portraits: 12x30's taller ratio wastes less space.",
"- Many small candid photos per spread: 12x36 allows a cleaner row of frames.",
"- A minimal album with one image per page: either works; 12x30 is often more economical.",
"## What clients usually choose",
"In practice, many Indian couples ask for 12x36 because it is the familiar 'Karizma album' size, while 12x30 is popular for budget packages, parent albums and smaller events such as engagements and birthdays. Showing both sizes as physical samples in the studio makes the choice easy for clients.",
"## Quick recommendation",
"Offer 12x36 as the main wedding album and 12x30 as the value option or for secondary albums. Keep separate template folders for each size so you never accidentally design on the wrong canvas, and confirm the size with the lab's order form before exporting."]),
'12x36-vs-14x40-album':('12x36 vs 14x40 Album Size: Which Is Better?',[
"14x40 is a larger panoramic album format offered by some labs as a premium option above the usual 12x36. If a client asks for 'the biggest album', you need to know what changes when you move up a size, both for the book and for your design work.",
"## Size and pixel dimensions",
"- **12x36:** closed page 12x18 inches; at 300 DPI a spread is 10800 x 3600 pixels.",
"- **14x40:** closed page 14x20 inches; at 300 DPI a spread is 12000 x 4200 pixels.",
"The aspect ratios are close (3:1 versus about 2.86:1), so 14x40 is slightly taller relative to its width. A 14x40 spread at 300 DPI has about 30 percent more pixels than a 12x36 spread.",
"## What the bigger size gives you",
"A 14x40 album has a strong presence on a table and lets hero photos print noticeably larger. It suits couples who want a showpiece album and studios that shoot with high-resolution cameras. The extra height is useful for full-length portraits of the couple in traditional outfits.",
"## What it costs you",
"- **Price:** labs charge more for larger sheets and larger covers. Get the current rate card from your lab.",
"- **Weight and handling:** a 14x40 album with many thick pages is heavy; consider the number of sheets.",
"- **Photo quality:** hero photos need more pixels. A photo that looks fine across half a 12x36 spread may look soft across half a 14x40 spread. Check scale percentages before you commit.",
"- **File size and speed:** larger layered PSDs need more RAM and storage.",
"## Can you convert 12x36 designs to 14x40?",
"Not by simply scaling. Because the proportions differ, scaling a 12x36 sheet to 14 inches tall makes it about 42 inches wide, which is too wide, and scaling to 40 inches wide leaves it too short. The correct approach is to start with a 14x40 canvas and either use templates built for that size or rearrange your 12x36 layout on the new canvas, extending backgrounds and repositioning frames. Our guide on [resizing album PSDs](/blog/resize-album-psd-to-another-size/) explains how.",
"## Templates for each size",
"Most free templates, including the majority on PSD Album, are made at 12x36 proportions because that is the most popular size. Check the pixel dimensions on each design page; if you work in 14x40 regularly, build a set of base canvases at 12000 x 4200 pixels and adapt layouts from the [12x36 wedding album collection](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) onto them.",
"## Which should you recommend?",
"- For most weddings, 12x36 is the sensible default: widely available, familiar to clients and well supported by templates.",
"- Offer 14x40 as a premium upgrade for clients who value a large showpiece and when your photos have the resolution to justify it.",
"- For parent albums and smaller events, go smaller rather than larger: 12x30 or 12x18.",
"Whatever you choose, confirm the exact sheet size, bleed and spine requirements with the lab before you start, and read the [12x36 album size guide](/blog/12x36-album-size-guide/) for print settings that apply to all panoramic formats."]),
'marriage-album-photo-size-guide':('Marriage Album Photo Size Guide: Common Sizes Explained',[
"When a client says 'normal album size', they could mean several different formats. Knowing the common marriage album sizes, and how many pixels each needs, helps you quote correctly, choose the right templates and avoid soft prints. This guide covers the sizes Indian studios use most often.",
"## How album sizes are written",
"Album sizes are usually written as the open spread: height x full width. A 12x36 album is 12 inches tall and 36 inches wide when opened, so each closed page is 12x18. Some labs write the closed page size instead, so always confirm which one an order form means.",
"## Common panoramic sizes",
"- **12x36** (closed 12x18): the most common wedding album size. 10800 x 3600 px at 300 DPI; 7200 x 2400 px at 200 DPI.",
"- **12x30** (closed 12x15): a slightly smaller, often cheaper panorama. 9000 x 3600 px at 300 DPI.",
"- **14x40** (closed 14x20): a premium large format. 12000 x 4200 px at 300 DPI.",
"- **12x24** (closed 12x12): square pages, popular for pre-wedding and modern albums. 7200 x 3600 px at 300 DPI.",
"## Other sizes you will see",
"- **18x24:** large-format sheets used for showpiece portraits and some premium albums. 7200 x 5400 px at 300 DPI.",
"- **12x18:** a single page or a compact spread, also the cover size of a 12x36 album. 5400 x 3600 px at 300 DPI.",
"- **8x24 and 10x30:** smaller panoramic sizes some labs offer for parent or mini albums.",
"## The pixel formula",
"Pixels = inches x DPI. For any size the lab offers, multiply both sides by the resolution you plan to print at. Divide pixels by DPI to work backwards: a 7200 pixel wide file prints 24 inches wide at 300 DPI or 36 inches wide at 200 DPI. Each design page on PSD Album shows its pixels and DPI and calculates the print size for you.",
"## Photo sizes inside the album",
"Photos inside an album do not have a fixed size; they fill frames of many sizes. What matters is that each photo has enough pixels for its frame. A frame 9 inches wide at 300 DPI needs a photo at least 2700 pixels wide. Most modern cameras easily exceed this, but heavily cropped images and phone screenshots often do not.",
"## Loose prints and frames",
"Studios also print individual photos for frames and gifts. Common print sizes include 4x6, 5x7, 8x10, 8x12, 12x18 and 16x24 inches. Our [photo print sizes guide](/blog/photo-print-sizes-pixels-dpi/) lists pixel sizes for each.",
"## Choosing the right size for a client",
"- Main wedding album: 12x36, or 14x40 as a premium option.",
"- Budget or parent albums: 12x30, 12x24 or smaller panoramic sizes.",
"- Pre-wedding albums: 12x24 square or 12x36.",
"- Birthday and baby albums: 12x30 or 12x36, depending on the number of photos.",
"Before designing, confirm the exact size, bleed and resolution on the lab's order form, then choose templates built at those proportions, such as the [12x36 wedding album PSDs](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) or [12x30 album PSDs](/12x30-album-psd/)."]),
'how-many-photos-in-a-wedding-album':('How Many Photos Should a Wedding Album Have?',[
"There is no single correct number of photos for a wedding album, but there is a correct way to decide it. Too few and the story feels incomplete; too many and every page looks crowded. This guide gives a practical way to plan photo counts for Indian wedding albums, where a single wedding can include several events over multiple days.",
"## Think in spreads, not total photos",
"Start from the number of sheets the client is paying for, then decide how many photos each spread can carry comfortably. A useful rule of thumb is one hero photo plus two to five supporting photos per spread. Some spreads deserve a single full-width image; others, like family groups, can carry more. So a 30-sheet album usually holds somewhere around 100 to 180 photos, depending on style. This is a design guideline, not an industry statistic; adjust it to your style.",
"## Style changes the count",
"- **Minimal or editorial albums:** fewer, larger photos, often one to three per spread.",
"- **Traditional albums:** more photos per spread, with rows of ritual and family images.",
"- **Mixed albums:** large hero spreads for key moments, denser spreads for rituals and guests.",
"Discuss the style with the couple before culling, ideally by showing printed sample albums.",
"## Allocate by event",
"Write down every event and how important it is to the couple. A typical South Indian wedding might include engagement, haldi or mangala snanam, mehendi, the muhurtham, rituals, couple portraits, family groups and the reception. Give the most important events the most spreads, and allow at least one spread for couple portraits and one for details and decor. Our [Tamil wedding album page order](/blog/tamil-wedding-album-page-order/) guide shows a full sequence.",
"## Leave room for family groups",
"Family group photos are often the most important pages for parents and grandparents. Plan dedicated spreads for them instead of squeezing them between ritual photos, and agree in advance which groups must appear. Missing a relative is a common reason for redesigns.",
"## How to cull to the right number",
"- Remove technical failures and near-duplicates.",
"- For each moment, keep the single best frame unless the sequence itself tells a story (for example the thali tying).",
"- Mark one hero image for every planned spread.",
"- Choose supporting images that add something different: a reaction, a detail, a wider view.",
"## When the client wants more photos",
"If a couple wants many more photos than the sheets allow, offer more sheets, a second album, or a slideshow and online gallery for the rest. Cramming too many photos into each spread makes the album look like a contact sheet.",
"## Templates by photo count",
"Choose templates that match your counts. A template with eight frames is a poor choice for a spread with three strong photos. Browse the [wedding album design PSD collection](/wedding-album-design-psd/) and note the frame count of each layout; keeping your own library sorted by frame count makes this fast.",
"## Related questions",
"For page count decisions, read [how many pages should a wedding album have](/blog/how-many-pages-in-a-wedding-album/). For layout balance, see [wedding album layout rules](/blog/wedding-album-layout-rules/)."]),
'how-many-pages-in-a-wedding-album':('How Many Pages Should a Wedding Album Have?',[
"Clients often ask how many pages a wedding album should have, and the honest answer is: enough to tell the story of the events they care about, and no more than their budget and the photos allow. Here is a practical way to decide, along with the terms that confuse many clients.",
"## Sheets, spreads and pages",
"In panoramic albums such as 12x36, one printed sheet usually forms one open spread (two facing pages). Labs often price albums by the number of sheets, while clients think in pages. Thirty sheets means about sixty page sides. Always state which unit you are quoting, both in your price list and on the order form, to avoid misunderstandings.",
"## Start from the events",
"List every event the album must cover and give each a number of sheets based on its importance. For example, an album might allocate a few sheets to preparations, several to the main ceremony and rituals, one or two to couple portraits, one or two to family groups and several to the reception. Add an opening sheet with the couple's best portrait and names, and a closing sheet. The total gives you a realistic page count.",
"## Then check the photos",
"Count the strong photos for each event. If an event has only a handful of good images, giving it many sheets will force you to use weak photos or repeat similar shots. If an event has many strong images, consider adding a sheet or moving extra photos to an online gallery. Our guide on [how many photos to put in an album](/blog/how-many-photos-in-a-wedding-album/) explains how to balance this.",
"## Budget and binding limits",
"More sheets increase the cost and the thickness of the book. Very thick albums are heavy and can strain the binding. Labs often have a minimum and maximum sheet count for each binding type and paper thickness; ask before quoting.",
"## Typical choices by album type",
"- **Main wedding album:** many studios offer packages with a fixed number of sheets and allow clients to add more.",
"- **Parent albums:** fewer sheets, focusing on family moments and key rituals.",
"- **Pre-wedding albums:** usually shorter, with larger photos and fewer per spread.",
"- **Reception or engagement albums:** short albums dedicated to one event.",
"These are common practices rather than fixed rules; your package structure should reflect your style and your clients.",
"## Offer upgrades, not cramming",
"When a couple wants to include more photos than the package allows, offer extra sheets at a clear price per sheet, or a second smaller album. This keeps the design clean and creates a fair upsell, instead of squeezing ten photos onto every spread.",
"## Plan with a template family",
"## A simple worked example",
"Suppose a couple wants coverage of the engagement, the wedding day and the reception, and the lab's package is 30 sheets. You might give one sheet to the opening portrait, four to the engagement, three to preparations, eight to the muhurtham and rituals, two to couple portraits, three to family groups, seven to the reception, one to details and one to a closing page. If the reception has fewer strong photos than planned, move a sheet to family groups. Numbers like these are only a starting point for the conversation with the couple.",
"Once the sheet count is agreed, choose one template family per event so the album feels consistent. Our [Karizma album PSD collection](/karizma-album-psd/) and [12x36 wedding album PSDs](/12x36-wedding-album-psd-free-download/) contain matching layouts in many styles. Write the plan (sheet number, event, hero photo) before you start designing, and get it approved by the couple."]),
'wedding-album-layout-rules':('Wedding Album Layout Rules Professionals Follow',[
"Good album layouts look effortless, but they follow a handful of rules that experienced designers apply almost automatically. Learning these rules makes any template look better and helps you adapt layouts to the photos you actually have.",
"## 1. One focal point per spread",
"Every spread should have one image that the eye lands on first, usually the largest and strongest photo. Supporting images are smaller and placed so they lead the eye back to the hero. If two photos are the same size and fighting for attention, make one bigger.",
"## 2. Respect the fold",
"Never put a face, a name or a key detail across the centre fold. Treat the left and right pages as two halves that work together, and keep a clear zone around the fold.",
"## 3. Use a grid and align everything",
"Frames should share edges and spacing. Use guides to set margins and gutters, and keep the gap between photos consistent across the album. Misaligned frames a few pixels apart look careless in print.",
"## 4. Margins create a premium look",
"Leave breathing room around the edges and between photos. Tight, edge-to-edge collages can work for energetic events, but most spreads look more expensive with generous margins. Keep important content inside the safe area so trimming does not cut it.",
"## 5. Match frame shapes to photo orientation",
"Put vertical photos in vertical frames and horizontal photos in horizontal frames. Cropping a landscape group shot into a tall frame removes people. If a template's frames do not match your photos, adjust the frames or choose another layout.",
"## 6. Tell the story in order",
"Arrange spreads chronologically by event, and within a spread, place photos so the sequence reads left to right. A thali-tying sequence or a reception entry reads naturally when the moments flow across the page.",
"## 7. Pair photos that belong together",
"Photos on the same spread should share lighting, colour and mood. A bright outdoor portrait next to a dark stage photo looks mismatched. If you must combine them, match their colour with clipped adjustment layers.",
"## 8. Vary the rhythm",
"Alternate dense spreads with simpler ones. After two spreads of ritual photos, give the viewer a single full-width portrait. This rhythm keeps the album interesting from start to finish.",
"## 9. Limit decoration",
"Ornaments, borders and textures should support the photos. Hide decorations that sit behind faces or draw attention away from the hero image. Often, using half the ornaments in a template looks more refined.",
"## 10. Consistency across the album",
"Use one font pair, a small colour palette and one template family per event. Consistency is what makes an album feel like one book rather than thirty separate designs.",
"## Applying the rules to templates",
"Templates from the [wedding album design PSD collection](/wedding-album-design-psd/) already follow many of these rules. Your job is to keep them intact while placing your photos: do not force a photo into a frame of the wrong orientation, do not fill every frame and do not add extra ornaments. Then check each spread against these rules before export, and run the [print-ready checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/)."]),
'wedding-album-design-ideas':('Wedding Album Design Ideas for Modern Studios',[
"Clients increasingly compare albums on social media before they choose a studio, so fresh design ideas matter. The best ideas are not gimmicks; they are ways of telling the couple's story more clearly. Here are practical ideas you can apply with standard templates and Photoshop.",
"## Open with a full-width hero",
"Start the album with one striking portrait across the whole spread, with the couple's names and date in clean type. It sets the tone better than a busy collage.",
"## Chapter openers for each event",
"Introduce each event with a spread that contains a large image and the event name: Nichayathartham, Mehendi, Muhurtham, Reception. Use the same typography and position each time so the album feels structured.",
"## Detail spreads",
"Group close-ups of jewellery, mehendi, invitations, decor and food on one spread. Detail images add texture and give the eye a rest between people-heavy pages.",
"## Sequence strips",
"For moments that unfold quickly, such as the thali tying, varmala or cake cutting, use a row of four to six equal frames across the spread. Sequences feel cinematic and tell the moment better than a single frame.",
"## Minimal pages with negative space",
"Place one photo on a plain background with generous margins. Minimal spreads look premium and modern, and they are ideal for strong portraits.",
"## Black-and-white accents",
"Converting one or two emotional moments, such as a parent's reaction, to black and white can add depth. Keep it consistent; random black-and-white images look like mistakes.",
"## Colour themes from the wedding itself",
"Pick two or three colours from the outfits or decor and use them in backgrounds and ornaments throughout the album. Our guide to [wedding album colour themes](/blog/wedding-album-colour-themes/) explains how.",
"## Regional touches, used lightly",
"Kolam patterns for Tamil albums, kasavu gold for Kerala, temple motifs for traditional ceremonies or geometric arches for Nikah albums add identity. Use them as accents rather than full-page decoration. See the [Tamil](/tamil-wedding-album-psd/), [Kerala](/kerala-wedding-album-psd/) and [Muslim wedding album](/muslim-wedding-album-psd/) collections for examples.",
"## Family tree or family spread",
"A dedicated spread with portraits of both families, labelled clearly, is valued by parents and grandparents for years.",
"## Behind-the-scenes and candid pages",
"Laughter during preparations, children playing, friends dancing: candid spreads make the album feel alive and balance formal portraits.",
"## Mixing panoramic and square albums",
"Offer a 12x36 main album with a matching 12x24 square pre-wedding or reception album. Using the same design language across both makes a strong package.",
"## Closing spread",
"End with a quiet portrait, the couple walking away, or a thank-you note. A thoughtful ending makes the album feel complete.",
"## Present ideas to clients visually",
"Clients understand layouts far better when they can see them. Keep a sample album or a tablet portfolio showing a few of these ideas, and share a low-resolution preview of their own spreads before printing. A preview PDF also gives you a clear record of what the couple approved; our guide on [making a wedding album PDF for client approval](/blog/wedding-album-pdf-for-client-approval/) shows how.",
"## Keep the design about the photos",
"Every idea above works only if the photos remain the focus. Choose clean layouts from the [Karizma album PSD collection](/karizma-album-psd/), follow the [layout rules](/blog/wedding-album-layout-rules/) and keep decoration in support of the story."]),
})
